1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,420
Here is a circle. Let's
zoom in on its edge.

2
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:05,520
Ten times. A hundred times.

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:08,780
A thousand. It stays perfectly smooth.

4
00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:10,460
That should be a little puzzling.

5
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:13,580
A circle contains infinitely many points,

6
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:16,260
and a computer has finite memory.

7
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:19,120
If you approximate the circle
with straight segments,

8
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:22,160
zooming in gives the game
away almost immediately.

9
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:24,140
So how is this circle stored?

10
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:28,160
The answer is: as eight pieces
of a special kind of curve,

11
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:31,040
each described by just four points.

12
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:35,000
And those same curves are behind every
outline you have seen in this series,

13
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,820
behind the letters in most fonts,

14
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:40,850
and behind almost every
drawing tool on your computer.

15
00:00:40,850 --> 00:00:44,030
To build that kind of curve,
we need only one tool,

16
00:00:44,030 --> 00:00:45,710
and you met it last episode:

17
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:46,870
linear interpolation.

18
00:00:46,870 --> 00:00:47,890
Lerp.

19
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:50,490
Take two points. Lerp between them,

20
00:00:50,490 --> 00:00:52,450
letting t run from zero to one,

21
00:00:52,450 --> 00:00:54,110
and you trace a straight line.

22
00:00:54,110 --> 00:00:55,410
Nothing surprising yet.

23
00:00:55,410 --> 00:00:56,390
Now take three points.

24
00:00:56,390 --> 00:01:00,070
Lerp along the first segment,
and along the second segment,

25
00:01:00,070 --> 00:01:01,590
both with the same t.

26
00:01:01,590 --> 00:01:03,550
That gives two moving points.

27
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:06,610
Now lerp between those
two, again with the same t.

28
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:08,390
This final point traces something new:

29
00:01:08,390 --> 00:01:11,450
a smooth curve that
starts at the first point,

30
00:01:11,450 --> 00:01:15,770
ends at the last, and bends toward the
middle point without ever touching it.

31
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:18,110
Do it once more, with four points.

32
00:01:18,110 --> 00:01:19,870
Three lerps on the first level,

33
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:21,910
two on the second, and one at the top.

34
00:01:21,910 --> 00:01:26,690
The single point that survives
traces a cubic Bézier curve.

35
00:01:26,690 --> 00:01:30,390
This construction is named
after Paul de Casteljau,

36
00:01:30,390 --> 00:01:34,410
who worked it out at the car maker
Citroën in the late nineteen fifties.

37
00:01:34,410 --> 00:01:40,090
Pierre Bézier, at Renault, popularised
the same curves for designing car bodies,

38
00:01:40,090 --> 00:01:42,010
and his name is the one that stuck.

39
00:01:42,010 --> 00:01:44,490
Scan this code to play with
the construction yourself.

40
00:01:44,490 --> 00:01:48,330
The four control points are in a list
called p at the top of the scene.

41
00:01:48,330 --> 00:01:49,970
Drag them around by editing the numbers,

42
00:01:49,970 --> 00:01:52,783
and watch the curve follow.

43
00:01:52,783 --> 00:01:55,123
The four points have different jobs.

44
00:01:55,123 --> 00:01:57,023
The first and last are anchors:

45
00:01:57,023 --> 00:01:58,943
the curve really passes through them.

46
00:01:58,943 --> 00:02:00,763
The middle two are handles.

47
00:02:00,763 --> 00:02:02,243
The curve never touches them,

48
00:02:02,243 --> 00:02:05,363
but it leaves the first anchor heading
straight toward the first handle,

49
00:02:05,363 --> 00:02:09,723
and arrives at the last anchor coming
from the direction of the second handle.

50
00:02:09,723 --> 00:02:13,803
Pull a handle further away, and the
curve leans harder in that direction.

51
00:02:13,803 --> 00:02:16,923
That is what makes these
curves so useful for drawing.

52
00:02:16,923 --> 00:02:18,923
You can chain them, end to end,

53
00:02:18,923 --> 00:02:22,003
so that one curve's last
anchor is the next one's first.

54
00:02:22,003 --> 00:02:25,423
And if the handles on either side
of that shared anchor line up,

55
00:02:25,423 --> 00:02:26,883
the join is smooth:

56
00:02:26,883 --> 00:02:28,283
no corner at all.

57
00:02:28,283 --> 00:02:29,643
Now we can look inside the circle.

58
00:02:29,643 --> 00:02:32,963
Manim builds it from eight cubic pieces,

59
00:02:32,963 --> 00:02:35,083
joined smoothly at eight anchors.

60
00:02:35,083 --> 00:02:37,023
That is thirty two points in total,

61
00:02:37,023 --> 00:02:38,963
counting each piece's four.

62
00:02:38,963 --> 00:02:40,863
The pieces are not exactly circular.

63
00:02:40,863 --> 00:02:43,443
But their error is far
smaller than a pixel,

64
00:02:43,443 --> 00:02:47,383
which is why the zoom in the
opening never revealed them.

65
00:02:47,383 --> 00:02:49,383
Why four points? Why not three,

66
00:02:49,383 --> 00:02:50,443
or ten?

67
00:02:50,443 --> 00:02:52,963
With three points, the quadratic curve,

68
00:02:52,963 --> 00:02:57,863
there's only one handle, and it controls
the direction at both ends at once.

69
00:02:57,863 --> 00:02:59,483
You can't choose them separately,

70
00:02:59,483 --> 00:03:01,223
and you can't make an S shape:

71
00:03:01,223 --> 00:03:04,403
a quadratic curve only ever bends one way.

72
00:03:04,403 --> 00:03:05,803
Four points, the cubic,

73
00:03:05,803 --> 00:03:10,523
is the smallest number that lets you
set each end's direction independently.

74
00:03:10,523 --> 00:03:13,503
That's exactly what you need
to join curves smoothly,

75
00:03:13,503 --> 00:03:16,483
and it's enough to make S
shapes within a single piece.

76
00:03:16,483 --> 00:03:18,343
You could go higher.

77
00:03:18,343 --> 00:03:20,523
Five, six, ten control points.

78
00:03:20,523 --> 00:03:23,423
But higher-degree curves
are harder to control:

79
00:03:23,423 --> 00:03:26,103
move one point, and
the whole curve shifts.

80
00:03:26,103 --> 00:03:28,863
It's far easier to
chain many cubic pieces,

81
00:03:28,863 --> 00:03:33,123
where moving a handle only changes
its own piece and its neighbour.

82
00:03:33,123 --> 00:03:37,043
That's why cubic curves became
the standard of design software.

83
00:03:37,043 --> 00:03:40,283
Some font formats use
quadratic pieces instead,

84
00:03:40,283 --> 00:03:44,423
but every quadratic curve can
be rewritten exactly as a cubic,

85
00:03:44,423 --> 00:03:48,500
so when Manim meets one, it
converts it and carries on.

86
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:52,820
In Manim, a shape made of
outlines is called a V mobject,

87
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:55,260
short for vectorised mobject.

88
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:59,040
And under the hood, every V
mobject is just a list of points,

89
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:01,120
read in groups of four:

90
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:03,860
anchor, handle, handle, anchor.

91
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:06,180
Even a straight line is stored this way.

92
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:08,320
Put both handles on the line itself,

93
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:10,880
and the cubic curve is perfectly straight.

94
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:13,400
So a square is four cubic curves,

95
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:16,080
sixteen points, which
happen to be straight.

96
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:19,700
This is a wonderfully
simple representation.

97
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:22,460
Squares, circles, stars, arrows,

98
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:26,980
graphs of functions and every letter of
every formula are all the same kind of

99
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:29,340
thing: a list of cubic curves.

100
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:33,500
And that uniformity is what
makes the next trick possible.

101
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:36,640
Let's return to the
question from last episode.

102
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:38,400
To morph one shape into another,

103
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:42,660
Manim pairs up their points and
moves each one in a straight line.

104
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:46,880
But a square has sixteen points
and the circle has thirty two.

105
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:50,080
Half of the circle's points
would have no partner.

106
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:52,060
The fix is beautiful.

107
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:54,400
Remember the de Casteljau construction?

108
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:57,580
Stop it at t equals one half,
and look at the scaffolding.

109
00:04:57,580 --> 00:05:01,780
The points along the way are exactly
the control points of two smaller cubic

110
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:06,000
curves, which together trace
the original curve perfectly.

111
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,720
So you can split any cubic curve into
two without changing its shape at all.

112
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:14,100
Manim splits the square's
four curves into eight.

113
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:16,200
The square looks exactly the same,

114
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:18,260
but it now has thirty two points,

115
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:21,440
and every point has a
partner on the circle.

116
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:25,700
Then the morph is exactly
what we saw in episode three:

117
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:27,740
each point travels in a straight line,

118
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:32,700
driven by alpha. The anchors at
the square's corners glide inward,

119
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:36,420
and the new anchors at the
middle of each edge bow outward,

120
00:05:36,420 --> 00:05:39,940
and the whole outline
relaxes into a circle.

121
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:45,100
This example calls align points directly
so you can see the new dots appear.

122
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:46,820
Normally you never need to.

123
00:05:46,820 --> 00:05:52,117
Every transform does this matching
automatically before it starts.

124
00:05:52,117 --> 00:05:54,557
Straight lines are only the default.

125
00:05:54,557 --> 00:05:59,617
Pass path arc to a transform, and every
point travels along an arc instead,

126
00:05:59,617 --> 00:06:02,077
turning by that angle as it goes.

127
00:06:02,077 --> 00:06:04,717
A quarter turn gives a gentle swing.

128
00:06:04,717 --> 00:06:07,057
A negative angle swings the other way.

129
00:06:07,057 --> 00:06:10,557
This also tells you something
about the morphs that look odd.

130
00:06:10,557 --> 00:06:13,357
Because the pairing goes in
order around the outline,

131
00:06:13,357 --> 00:06:16,177
two shapes that start their
outlines in different places,

132
00:06:16,177 --> 00:06:17,937
or travel in opposite directions,

133
00:06:17,937 --> 00:06:20,697
can make points cross
over each other mid-morph.

134
00:06:20,697 --> 00:06:23,177
The outline appears to twist.

135
00:06:23,177 --> 00:06:23,997
When you see that,

136
00:06:23,997 --> 00:06:28,077
the fix is usually to rotate or reverse
one of the shapes so their starting

137
00:06:28,077 --> 00:06:30,100
points line up.

138
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:33,280
One more payoff. How
does Create draw a curve,

139
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:35,820
gradually, as if with a pen?

140
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:39,800
At every frame, it shows only
the first part of the outline:

141
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:43,340
the curve cut off at some
fraction of the way along.

142
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:46,480
And cutting a cubic
curve at any fraction is,

143
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:49,440
once again, de Casteljau's construction.

144
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:54,520
The same lerps that built the curve also
let Manim draw it one sliver at a time.

145
00:06:54,520 --> 00:06:56,180
You can build curves yourself.

146
00:06:56,180 --> 00:07:00,660
Cubic Bézier takes exactly four points:

147
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:03,600
anchor, handle, handle, anchor.

148
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:07,220
And if you would rather just say
"go through these points smoothly",

149
00:07:07,220 --> 00:07:11,560
a V mobject's set points smoothly
method works out handles for you,

150
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:13,960
so that the joins have no corners.

151
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:16,500
The same machinery
answers another question:

152
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:19,240
where is the point halfway
along this outline?

153
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:22,680
Point from proportion measures along
the curves and returns that point,

154
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:27,300
and Move along path uses it to
slide an object along any outline,

155
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:31,200
a circle, a polygon, or a
curve you drew yourself.

156
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:33,600
Scan this code, move the handles around,

157
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:38,580
and you will build your intuition for
Bézier curves faster than any explanation

158
00:07:38,580 --> 00:07:40,483
can.

159
00:07:40,483 --> 00:07:41,803
Let's put it together.

160
00:07:41,803 --> 00:07:45,623
A cubic Bézier curve is
what you get when you lerp,

161
00:07:45,623 --> 00:07:48,343
and lerp again, and lerp once more.

162
00:07:48,343 --> 00:07:51,743
Anchors are where it goes;
handles are how it leans.

163
00:07:51,743 --> 00:07:54,203
Chain these curves, and
you can draw anything,

164
00:07:54,203 --> 00:07:58,323
so Manim stores every outline as a
list of cubic curves in groups of four

165
00:07:58,323 --> 00:08:02,783
points. To morph two shapes, it
splits curves until the counts match,

166
00:08:02,783 --> 00:08:04,563
then pairs points and moves them.

167
00:08:04,563 --> 00:08:09,123
And the very same construction lets
Create draw a curve one sliver at a time.

168
00:08:09,123 --> 00:08:12,663
Next time, we put this to work on
the most intricate outlines of all:

169
00:08:12,663 --> 00:08:14,477
letters, numbers and equations.
